  "findings": {
    "direction": "Correct. The prior is inverse depth (disparity): mean Spearman +0.56 vs 1/z and -0.56 vs z on Winchester, +0.93 / -0.93 on the bedroom. The losses treat it as disparity (Pearson on rendered inverse depth), so no sign bug.",
    "scale_shift": "Bedroom: an affine fit in inverse depth explains it well (R^2 0.91-0.996 on 6/8 frames; one sparse 98-point frame is monotonic but not affine, R^2 0.004). Winchester: inconsistent per frame. 4/8 frames fit (R^2 0.60-0.995), 4/8 barely or not at all (R^2 0.001-0.26; one frame anti-correlated, Spearman -0.08). A fixed-weight Pearson loss on those frames pulls the geometry towards a wrong surface, which matches the bowl-shaped depth in the Winchester sanity panel and the -3.6 dB collapse.",
    "alignment": "A real but small bug. The prior was computed on the raw distorted frame and only resized, while nerfstudio trains on the undistorted ROI crop. Lookup error: Winchester (k1 0.0049) mean 1.3 px, p95 2.0 px of 1600 (0.13%); bedroom (k1 0.0298) mean 3.6 px, p95 4.9 px of 900 (0.55%). Fit change from the misalignment: R^2 0.478 -> 0.478 (Winchester), 0.807 -> 0.802 (bedroom). Not the cause of the collapse. Fixed anyway: prior_alignment.align_map_to_nerfstudio warps + crops each map exactly like nerfstudio (full-res parity test error 4e-4 vs 0.17 for resize-only), applied in modal _build_depth_prior; prior.json records aligned_to.",
    "root_cause_ranking": [
      "per-frame prior reliability (Winchester 4/8 frames unusable) with no gating",
      "loss weights too strong / too early (Pearson 0.2 from step 500 at 30K/400K)",
      "undistortion misalignment (minor, fixed)"
    ]
  },
